  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nMonday Night through Tuesday Night\n\nFrom Monday night into Tuesday, a shortwave trough will move along \nthe US/Canadian border, working to flatten the upper level \nridge over the Upper Mississippi River. As this occurs, a cold \nfront will move slowly southeast through the region on Tuesday. \nFor areas along and north of the Interstate 90 corridor, the \ntiming of this front will only be favorable for isolated to \nscattered showers and storms. During the afternoon and evening, \n0-1 km CAPE will be up to 1500 J/kg along and ahead of the cold\nfront in northeast Iowa, and southwest and central Wisconsin. \nWith the better 0-3 km and 0-6 km shear located well in the wake\nof the front, organized severe weather look unlikely at this \ntime.\n\nLow temperatures will range from the lower 50s to mid-60s on Monday \nand Tuesday nights. High temperatures will range from the mid-70s to \nlower 80s on Tuesday.\n\nWednesday into Thursday\n\nAnother shortwave trough will shift east over the Dakotas into\nthe Upper Mississippi River Valley through midweek. The\nassociated front will become quasi-stationary across northeast \nIowa and southwest Wisconsin on Wednesday before departing south\nof the area on Wednesday night through Thursday. Again there is\nlittle shear located across the area, so organized convection \nlooks unlikely at this time.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the 70s on Wednesday. Low temperatures \nwill range from the upper 40s to upper 50s on Wednesday night. High \ntemperatures will range from the mid-70s to lower 80s on Thursday.",
